nt tell me about sales she  recognizes herself totally in the  characters I'm not being indiscreet or  anything and the other thing I wanted to  ask was what you said before I hope I  don't seem impertinent here but that you  were kind of when you wrote for the  colony you said you wrote horrible  things about men and I just wanted to  ask you if your your feminist themes or  any other themes that matter have  mellowed or changed that's very kind so  graceful aged  thank you for the question thank you I  have not mellowed down I have even more  I'm even more passionate you know I've  always been a feminist and that doesn't  mean that I don't like men I like them  therefore I want to improve them I  haven't I haven't mellowed down with age  at all I have always been as passionate  as I am today and I I don't think that  that age will do much change in that  sense I realize now that I have the same  beliefs and ideas that I had when I was  young and I would give my life for the  same causes that I did then and I'm just  as passionate I would again fall in love  the way I felt in love with Willie I  fell in love with Lily before that was  long ago but I could still do the same  crazy things I mean there's nothing  different the only thing is that now you  can take the risk there's nothing to  lose it's great ok the next question  over here one more question this one on  this one thank you for speaking to us  tonight I just wanted to ask you when  you said that right now about happiness  that there's something more relevant or  something more important than happiness  that surprised me and I wanted to ask  you what you were referring to what is  more important than happiness well when  I was growing up happiness was  irrelevant my grandparents would have  been surprised to learn I mean horrified  to learn that people paid good money to  go to therapy to get over there and  happiness to pay for that would have  been unthinkable life was supposed to be  hard and difficult and you get over you  have to get over the obstacles and have  a life in spite of unhappiness and  difficulties and there were moments to  celebrate and those moments were  cherished and and we did celebrate them  a lot but it life was not about being  happy it was about honor about service  about community about being responsible  about leaving a legacy for your children  to be proud of that kind of stuff was  what I was I grew up on  and and that's the kind of stuff like  that still sort of guides my my life and  I was never pampered I the idea that you  can open a faucet and hot water comes  out what's wonderful I mean when when  you grow up with cold water it's  fantastic to have hard water but you can  live without it and so that's the point  with happiness I i think that i have i  have had a wonderful full life and in my  life there has been a lot of sorrow and  losses and abandonment and pain and  violence and grief and a lot of success  and love and passion and lust and  chocolate and good stuff and that's what  it is about but if you ask me do you  want to be happy no I'm not looking for  happiness I want to be our own a good  writer I want to be effective I want to  connect with people I want to change  things I want to serve to give that's  what I want to do but be happy what  stupid thing to be happy  I can't hear you what was your favorite  bucks right what was my favorite yes  what your favorite book to write my  favorite book to write I don't have well  that's you're the first person who asked  that question that way because people  ask what is your favorite book but they  don't say what is your favorite book to  write which it's quite a different  question and I always say that the most  important book that I have ever written  is paola because it saved my life at a  point when I really wanted to commit  suicide I was so depressed that I just  couldn't bear life because my daughter  had died and i wrote this memoir and it  saved my life and it brought me back to  my body and to be the person I am and it  was it was a cathodic book and an  important book but the most fun book to  ride was the first one the house of the  spirits because I didn't know what I was  doing I had that I didn't have the  slightest idea I was so innocent I  didn't know that there was a world out  there of literary critics of professors  of publishers of agents of contracts I  didn't know that I would be asked the  same question over and over again in  thousand times that I would be compared  to Gabriel Garcia Marquez that I didn't  know any of that I just wrote and I let  the story flow and I didn't know if it  was ever going to be published and I  didn't care much and nothing had  happened yet so everything that happened  with that book one step at a time  brought so much joy that I think that's  the most joyful experience of all also  it was a hard time in my life I was  going through a terrible marriage I had  a terrible job my kids were growing up  and they were going to the University  and I was losing my kids so and I felt  that I was getting old and I hadn't done  anything with my life so the joy of  finding a voice is something that that  is unique and I think that it only  happens with the first book  thank you so much thank you  isabel allende is author of the novels  portrait in sepia daughter of fortune  and the house of spirits her new memoir  my invented country is published by  Harper Collins on the web at  HarperCollins com next week on public  lives former news anchor Robert MacNeil  chronicles his personal
 

